"""the rules the engine starts with"""

rule base standard where

-- **Every callable here carries its signature** (MS5 phase 93, his ruling —
-- "that's why we have annotations"). None is needed: inference works each one
-- out, and `InstralInferTests` asserts that what is written here is exactly
-- what it works out, so a signature cannot drift from its rule.

-- **@fresh-universe@ is a FUNCTION now** (MS5 phase 89): it was one op doing
-- two things — mint a level, then wrap it — and the two are `fresh-level` and
-- `universe-at`. Every use of the word below is unchanged.
--
-- **A function and not a rule**, and that is not a preference: it has no head,
-- and a headless rule matches everywhere, so `:matches` would offer it at every
-- focus and `prove` would run it. That is phase 49c's `enter-binders` lesson —
-- a helper wants a head that is TRUE, and there is no true head here because it
-- reads nothing about the development at all.
fresh-universe : Core
fresh-universe = do
  l = fresh-level
  u = universe-at l
  return u

attack : ()
rule attack :- when focus-is-hole do prim-attack

try-core : Core -> ()
rule try-core t :- when focus-is-hole do prim-try t

abandon : ()
rule abandon :- when focus-is-hole do prim-abandon

intro : ()
rule intro :- when focus-is-guess goal-type-is-pi do prim-lambda

rule intro :- when focus-is-guess goal-type-is-let do prim-let

solve : ()
rule solve :- when focus-is-guess do prim-solve

regret : ()
rule regret :- when focus-is-guess do prim-regret

eliminate-core : Core -> ()
rule eliminate-core t :- when focus-is-hole do prim-eliminate t

prove : ()
rule prove :- when focus-is-hole do prim-prove

rule prove :- when focus-is-guess do prim-prove

fill : Core -> ()
rule fill t :- when focus-is-hole
  do n = fresh-name "refined"
     ; x = define n t
     ; s = typeof x
     ; g = goal
     ; unify-into s g
     ; prim-try x

unify-refine-core : Core -> ()
rule unify-refine-core t :- when focus-is-hole
  do fill t
     ; solve

apply-core : Core -> ()
rule apply-core f :- when focus-is-hole
  do s = prim-apply f
     ; unify-refine-core s

-- fit-core — apply-core's searching sibling, and MS2 phase 27's deliverable.
--
-- apply-core SATURATES: prim-apply reads the whole telescope in Haskell and
-- claims a hole for every argument at once. fit-core does not know the arity
-- and does not ask. It tries the spine as it stands, and if that does not fit
-- the goal it claims ONE more argument and tries again.
--
-- The two clauses are ORDERED and NOT EXCLUSIVE, which is the design rather
-- than an accident of writing: clause 1 comes first, so this is
-- minimal-arity-first, and it stops as soon as the spine fits rather than
-- guessing past a metavariable. Nothing sequences them — the engine builds a
-- choice point because both heads pass, and clause 1 FAILING is what reaches
-- clause 2. The `backtracking to` lines are it backing off through the arities,
-- and `:choices` shows it mid-search.
--
-- His design, 2026-08-24, unchanged. It needed no op MS4 had not already built
-- for `spine-arguments`, which is this same recursion with a surface term
-- saying how many arguments there are instead of the search finding out.
fit-core : Core -> ()
rule fit-core f :- when focus-is-hole
  do fill f
     ; solve

rule fit-core f :- when focus-is-hole
  do n  = fresh-name "a"
     ; f2 = apply-next f n
     ; call fit-core f2

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The asking half of the three component tactics (MS5 phase 62b)
--
-- claim, assume and quantify are ops and take a name and a type. Written with
-- the type alone they are these rules, which ask for the name and then run the
-- op. His design, 2026-09-11: "the normal claim is operation (primitive and
-- built in), the unary claim is a rule" — the arity picks the clause, which is
-- what the engine already does for rules, and the driver compiles nothing.
--
-- Until this phase the REPL read `claim A : Type₀` with a grammar of its own
-- and built one of two instruction sequences in Haskell depending on whether a
-- name was written. That is the special-casing this replaces.
--
-- The prompt cannot quote the type back, because nothing in instral renders a
-- term — ms5/CLOSEOUT.md 1.
--
-- NO HEAD, and that is the honest answer rather than the tidy one. The tidy one
-- was focus-is-component, written first to keep these out of the core
-- fragment's match list — and it is FALSE: the ops insert above the focus
-- through Cursor.insertAbove, which works from inside a core term too, so
-- `claim "k" ⌜ Nat ⌝` succeeds there. A head that refused what the op it wraps
-- allows would make the two clauses of one word disagree about where the word
-- applies. The enter-binders lesson (MS4 phase 49c) is that a head must be
-- TRUE, not that every rule must have one.
--
-- So they are offered wherever there is a focus, the core fragment included.
-- That costs a row in `:matches` and nothing else: `dispatch` runs no rule that
-- takes parameters, so `prove` cannot reach them.
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------

claim : Core -> ()
rule claim ty :-
  do n = ask "name for the new hole?" name
     ; claim n ty
     ; t = name-text n
     ; m = concat "claimed " t
     ; say m

assume : Core -> ()
rule assume ty :-
  do n = ask "name for the assumption?" name
     ; assume n ty
     ; t = name-text n
     ; m = concat "assumed " t
     ; say m

quantify : Core -> ()
rule quantify ty :-
  do n = ask "name for the ∀-binder?" name
     ; quantify n ty
     ; t = name-text n
     ; m = concat "quantified " t
     ; say m

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Elaboration, one clause per surface node (MS4 step 2)
--
-- Every clause names the node it is for, so at most one head matches any term
-- and a call to elaborate has nothing to choose between. **The three
-- application clauses were the exception until MS8 phase 170** — the binary one
-- asked only that the arguments be explicit, so it matched a name-headed spine
-- too and was tried whenever the name-headed clause failed. It is now the
-- clause for a head that is neither a name nor an elim, and there is no
-- exception left: elaboration does not backtrack.
--
-- **Elaboration is entirely here as of MS4 phase 49f.** There is no
-- prim-elaborate any more and there is no Haskell elaborator behind it; what
-- follows is the whole of E⟦·⟧.

-- E⟦x⟧ = FILL x ; SOLVE — Brady's variable case.
elaborate : Surface -> ()
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-name t)
  do w = surface-name t
     ; x = resolve-name w
     ; fill x
     ; solve

-- E⟦"ab"⟧, E⟦'c'⟧, E⟦3⟧, E⟦/[a-z]/⟧ — a literal is its own term (MS6 phases
-- 97c and 100). Nothing has to be looked up, so the term is ready; apply-core
-- claims a hole for each argument its type asks for and then fills and solves.
-- A string's type asks for none, so for it this is exactly fill; solve. A regex
-- literal's is ∀ (T : Type₀) -> Token T, so it becomes /[a-z]/ ?T and solve
-- unifies Token ?T with the goal — which is where T comes from (his ruling,
-- 2026-09-19). A T nothing determines stays an unsolved hole; nothing is
-- defaulted.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-literal t)
  do l = surface-literal t
     ; apply-core l

-- E⟦LC`( λ x : ι . x )`⟧ — a term of an object language (MS6 phase 104). The
-- text is parsed with LC's own grammar and the reading is written out as the
-- constructor application it denotes, which is then elaborated like any other
-- application. A ${e} in the region is an ordinary surface term standing where
-- its slot is, so it is elaborated there, at the constructor argument's type,
-- and nothing here has to know it was written as a splice.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-object t)
  do a = object-term t
     ; call elaborate a

-- E⟦Typeₙ⟧ — the level is written, so the term is too.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-universe t)
  do u = surface-universe t
     ; fill u
     ; solve

-- E⟦Type⟧ — typical ambiguity: the level is a fresh meta, unification decides.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-universe-open t)
  do u = fresh-universe
     ; fill u
     ; solve

-- E⟦_⟧ — "those elaborate just by not elaborating". The hole is already there.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-placeholder t) do

-- E⟦?foo⟧ — a named placeholder is a real hole, which the focus already is.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-hole t) do

-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- E⟦induction t of { p -> e ; … }⟧ — the splitting tree (MS9)
--
-- One method per constructor, each specialised by its equations, each arm's
-- body elaborated under the binders its pattern names. Coverage is the engine's
-- own backtracking: find-arm walks the arms and try-arm's two clauses are a
-- choice point, so an arm that does not fit is followed by the next one.
--
-- A FORCED ARGUMENT HAS NO BINDER, AND ITS NAME BECOMES A DEFINITION. An
-- equation can determine one of the constructor's own arguments — splitting
-- v : Vec A (succ n) solves vcons's own n against the outer one — and then
-- the subgoal has one binder fewer than the pattern has names. specialise says
-- which positions went and to what, so intro-pattern walks the pattern against
-- that report: a survivor is a prim-lambda, and a forced position is a `define`
-- equal to the term it was forced to. HIS RULING, 2026-10-19: a forced position
-- may be named, nothing is prevented, and the dot notation stays optional.
--
-- Forcing is the normal case rather than a corner — it is what every
-- inversion lemma does. The term a position is forced to is always written in
-- the context the split was written in and never in the method's own binders,
-- which is what makes `define` above the hole well scoped.
-- --------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A pattern argument is a name or a wildcard. A NESTED one is a second split
-- and is not this phase's: with no clause for it, `no-nesting` is a refusal
-- that names itself.
no-nesting : List a -> ()
rule no-nesting [] :- do

-- One binding per pattern argument, under the name the user wrote, answering
-- the hole that is left. The shape intro-binders uses for a λ group, and for
-- the same reason: counting moves from the top of a guess is only right while
-- the binders are the only components in the chain.
--
-- THE PATTERN AND THE FORCED REPORT ARE WALKED IN STEP, which is also what
-- checks that they agree: specialise answers one entry per constructor
-- argument, so two lists running out together is the only shape with a clause.
--
-- `none` is a binder the subgoal still has, so prim-lambda takes it and answers
-- the hole underneath. `some v` is a position an equation determined, so there
-- is no binder to take and the name is bound as a definition equal to v —
-- which leaves the hole where it was, `define` extending the context above it
-- rather than consuming it. A wildcard in a forced position asks for no name,
-- so nothing is bound at all.
intro-pattern : List Pattern -> List (Option Core) -> Core -> Core
rule intro-pattern [] [] k :- do return k
rule intro-pattern [p, ...ps] [none, ...fs] k :- when (pattern-is-wildcard p)
  do w = fresh-name "u"
     ; k2 = prim-lambda w
     ; r = intro-pattern ps fs k2
     ; return r
rule intro-pattern [p, ...ps] [none, ...fs] k :- when (pattern-is-name p)
  do sub = pattern-arguments p
     ; no-nesting sub
     ; w = pattern-head p
     ; k2 = prim-lambda w
     ; r = intro-pattern ps fs k2
     ; return r
rule intro-pattern [p, ...ps] [(some v), ...fs] k :- when (pattern-is-wildcard p)
  do r = intro-pattern ps fs k
     ; return r
rule intro-pattern [p, ...ps] [(some v), ...fs] k :- when (pattern-is-name p)
  do sub = pattern-arguments p
     ; no-nesting sub
     ; w = pattern-head p
     ; define w v
     ; r = intro-pattern ps fs k
     ; return r

-- WHICH HYPOTHESES AN ARM IS HANDED, and the one place the two words differ
-- once a method is in hand: `induction` hands one per recursive argument, and
-- `case` hands none, because caseD's methods are the eliminator's minus exactly
-- these. Two clauses with complementary tests, so no choice point.
ihs-for : Surface -> Name -> List Name
rule ihs-for t c :- when (split-is-induction t)
  do r = constructor-recursive-arguments c
     ; return r
rule ihs-for t c :- when (split-is-cases t)
  do return []

-- One induction hypothesis per recursive argument, named AFTER a fresh-name of
-- the argument it is about. `ihs-for` is what decides the list.
--
-- THE FRESHENING IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT TIDINESS. The argument's own binder is
-- already in the development by the time this runs, so using its name here
-- SHADOWS it and the arm's body resolves to the hypothesis instead. Where the
-- motive does not depend on the target the two have the same type, so nothing
-- fails and the proof is simply built out of the wrong variable.
intro-ihs : List Name -> Core -> Core
rule intro-ihs [] k :- do return k
rule intro-ihs [n, ...ns] k :-
  do h = fresh-name n
     ; k2 = prim-lambda h
     ; r = intro-ihs ns k2
     ; return r

-- THE AMBIENT ENTRIES THE SPLIT GENERALISED, BOUND UNDER THE NAMES THEY ALREADY
-- HAD — MS9 phase 191, `~6dpg5`. A motive abstracts the target's indices, so a
-- variable bound before the target was never refined; the motive now quantifies
-- a fresh copy of each such entry after the target, and these are the copies.
--
-- THE SHADOWING IS THE WHOLE POINT, and it is the exact opposite of
-- `intro-ihs`'s freshening one rule up. The ambient entry is still in scope and
-- still has its old type; the copy has the refined one. Binding the copy under
-- the ambient name is what makes the arm's body read `s0` and get the refined
-- `s0` — which is what Agda and Idris do by refining the context in place, and
-- what the equation handed to the user was standing in for.
--
-- IT IS `intro-pattern` WITHOUT THE PATTERN, and it walks the same two lists:
-- `none` is a copy the subgoal still binds, so `prim-lambda` takes it under the
-- ambient name, and that is the refinement — `s0 : Step p p'` reaching the arm
-- as `s0 : Step true p'`.
--
-- A SOLVED COPY IS BOUND TO NOTHING, AND THE NAME KEEPS ITS AMBIENT MEANING.
-- The copy is an index position the split has forced — `p` becoming `true` — and
-- the goal and every surviving copy's type already carry it, because the
-- specialiser substituted it before handing the subgoal over. What is missing is
-- only a NAME for it inside the arm, and there is nowhere in today's surface for
-- the user to have asked for one: an outer position is not in an arm's pattern.
-- Binding `p` to `true` here would be the elaborator redefining a name the user
-- bound outside the split, unasked. A dot pattern is the syntax that asks —
-- `~hzcgd`'s option A — and the term the define would use is reported for it.
--
-- IT RUNS AFTER THE PATTERN AND AFTER THE HYPOTHESES, and that is not a
-- preference: the copies stand after both in the method.
--
-- THE SECOND PARAMETER IS `List (Option a)`, NOT `List (Option Core)`: no clause
-- looks inside the `some`, so inference generalises it and a narrower written
-- signature is refused. `intro-pattern` writes `Option Core` because its own
-- clauses `define` the term.
intro-refined : List Name -> List (Option a) -> Core -> Core
rule intro-refined [] [] k :- do return k
rule intro-refined [n, ...ns] [none, ...fs] k :-
  do k2 = prim-lambda n
     ; r = intro-refined ns fs k2
     ; return r
rule intro-refined [n, ...ns] [(some v), ...fs] k :-
  do r = intro-refined ns fs k
     ; return r

-- The arm's pattern must name as many variables as the constructor takes
-- arguments. Two lists and no arithmetic: they are walked together, so a
-- mismatch is a list that runs out first and there is no clause for it.
same-length : List a -> List b -> ()
rule same-length [] [] :- do
rule same-length [p, ...ps] [n, ...ns] :- do same-length ps ns

-- THE SEARCH FOR AN ARM IS SEPARATE FROM ELABORATING IT, and that is not
-- tidiness. The obvious shape — one rule that both matches the name and
-- elaborates the body — makes the two clauses a choice point over the whole
-- arm, so a body that fails to elaborate is indistinguishable from an arm whose
-- name did not match: the search walks on, runs out of arms, and reports
-- itself. MS8 phase 170 found the same thing in the application clauses and the
-- lesson is in DECISIONS.md. So `pick` answers with the arm and can fail for
-- one reason only, and the caller's elaboration failure is the caller's.
-- IT ANSWERS WITH AN OPTION, and running out of arms is `none` rather than no
-- clause at all. Two reasons, both from his ruling of 2026-10-20. A case the
-- specialiser closed may legitimately have no arm, so the caller has to be able
-- to ask without failing. And where a case did survive, running out of arms is
-- COVERAGE failing, which `case-needs-an-arm` now says in its own name — until
-- this phase it was reported as `no clause of arm-for applies here`, which named
-- a rule in the shipped base instead of the missing arm.
arm-for : Surface -> Name -> Option (Pattern, Option Surface)
rule arm-for t c :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-no-arms t)
  do z = none
     ; return z
rule arm-for t c :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-arms t)
  do p = split-arm-pattern t
     ; w = pattern-head p
     ; b = split-arm-body t
     ; r = split-tail t
     ; a = pick w c p b r
     ; return a

-- Two clauses, MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE by a pair of complementary tests, so this
-- search records no choice point at all. With one test and an ordering both
-- clauses match when the names agree, and then any failure after the search
-- backtracks into it, walks on through the arms and reports the search rather
-- than the real error — phase 170's finding, one level over. Running out of
-- arms is coverage failing, and there is no clause for it.
pick : Name -> Name -> Pattern -> Option Surface -> Surface -> Option (Pattern, Option Surface)
rule pick w c p b r :- when (same-name w c) do a = some (p, b) ; return a
rule pick w c p b r :- when (different-name w c) do a = arm-for r c ; return a

-- AN ARM FOR A CASE THAT SURVIVED NEEDS A BODY. An empty arm — `‹pattern› ->`
-- and nothing after it — is how a user says the case cannot arise, and this case
-- did not: the method is here, so there is something to prove and the arm is what
-- proves it. One clause for `some` and none for `none`, which is
-- `case-must-vanish`'s idiom mirrored, so the refusal names the user's complaint
-- rather than a rule that went looking.
--
-- IT ELABORATES THE BODY ITSELF rather than handing it back, and that is what
-- keeps the type `Option Surface -> ()`: a rule that only unwrapped the option
-- would be inferred `Option a -> a`, since nothing in it says what the body is,
-- and a polymorphic result makes it an answer to `:produces Core`. One clause,
-- so there is no choice point to backtrack into — the hazard `pick`'s comment
-- describes needs two.
--
-- SO A MISSING BODY IS REFUSED WHERE A BAD BODY IS, after the arm's binders are
-- introduced and at the point the body was wanted, which the engine unwinds the
-- same way.
arm-needs-body : Option Surface -> ()
rule arm-needs-body (some b) :- do call elaborate b

-- The arm, under the bindings its pattern names and the copies the split
-- refined. The three groups are introduced in the order the method binds them:
-- the constructor's arguments, then the hypotheses, then the generalised copies.
elaborate-arm : Surface -> Name -> Core -> List (Option Core) -> (List Name, Int) -> List (Option Core) -> (Pattern, Option Surface) -> ()
rule elaborate-arm t c sub forced (gens, k) refined (p, ob) :-
  do as = pattern-arguments p
     ; args = constructor-arguments c
     ; same-length as args
     ; goto sub
     ; prim-attack
     ; into
     ; k0 = here
     ; back
     ; k1 = intro-pattern as forced k0
     ; ihs = ihs-for t c
     ; k2 = intro-ihs ihs k1
     ; k3 = intro-refined gens refined k2
     ; goto k3
     ; arm-needs-body ob
     ; goto sub
     ; solve

-- A CASE THAT SURVIVED NEEDS AN ARM — coverage, and this is where it is said.
-- One clause for `some` and none for `none`, so the refusal names the user's
-- complaint: there is a method left and no arm was written for it.
case-needs-an-arm : Surface -> Name -> Core -> List (Option Core) -> (List Name, Int) -> List (Option Core) -> Option (Pattern, Option Surface) -> ()
rule case-needs-an-arm t c sub forced m refined (some a) :-
  do elaborate-arm t c sub forced m refined a

-- AND A CASE THAT VANISHED TAKES NO BODY — the other half of his ruling of
-- 2026-10-20. Two clauses, and between them they are every legal shape: no arm
-- at all, or an arm that stops at the arrow. An arm with a body is the user
-- claiming the case is reachable against a proof that it is not, and there is no
-- clause for it.
-- **`Option (a, Option b) -> ()`** is what inference works out and therefore what
-- is written: neither clause looks at the pattern or at what a body would be, so
-- nothing here says what an arm is made of. `case-must-vanish` is written the same
-- way for the same reason.
vanished-case-takes-no-body : Option (a, Option b) -> ()
rule vanished-case-takes-no-body none :- do
rule vanished-case-takes-no-body (some (p, none)) :- do

-- WRITING NO ARMS IS THE CLAIM THAT EVERY CASE VANISHES, so a method that
-- survives one is refused here. `case-must-vanish` has a clause for `none` and
-- none for `some`, which is the `no-nesting` idiom: the refusal names itself,
-- and it names the user's complaint rather than the three rules further down
-- that would have gone looking for an arm there is no syntax for.
case-must-vanish : Option a -> ()
rule case-must-vanish none :- do

-- A method with no subgoal is the impossible case vanishing: the specialiser
-- filled it from a conflicting equation and claimed nothing, so there is
-- nothing to elaborate into and the arm has no body to elaborate.
--
-- AN ARM FOR A VANISHED CASE IS WRITTEN WITH NO BODY — `‹pattern› ->` and
-- nothing after it, his ruling of 2026-10-20. A body written here is the user
-- claiming the case is reachable against a proof that it is not, and it is
-- refused by `vanished-case-takes-no-body`; `induction d of { }` already says the
-- same thing one level out, which is why no keyword was reserved for it.
--
-- NOTHING IS SAID ON THE WAY PAST. This clause said *a case vanished: its arm is
-- unreachable* until phase 190, and a completed load discarded it — nothing is
-- reported on a successful path anywhere in the system, and a mistake means the
-- load is not successful. His ruling, 2026-10-20.
--
-- TWO QUESTIONS, ASKED SEPARATELY: did the method vanish, and did the user
-- write arms. The arm-less clause is first and its test is complementary to the
-- other two, so this search records no choice point — `pick`'s shape, for
-- `pick`'s reason.
one-case : Surface -> Name -> Option Core -> List (Option Core) -> (List Name, Int) -> List (Option Core) -> ()
rule one-case t c sub forced m refined :- when (split-has-no-arms t)
  do case-must-vanish sub
rule one-case t c none forced m refined :- when (split-has-arms t)
  do oa = arm-for t c
     ; vanished-case-takes-no-body oa
rule one-case t c (some sub) forced m refined :- when (split-has-arms t)
  do oa = arm-for t c
     ; case-needs-an-arm t c sub forced m refined oa

-- EVERY ARM THE USER WROTE WAS ASKED FOR — HIS RULING, 2026-10-20, and the two
-- walks below are the half of it that nothing else can see. Elaboration asks the
-- datatype's constructors for arms, one at a time; these ask the other
-- direction, because an arm nobody asks for is never looked at and the user is
-- told nothing. The three ways that happens are a head that is not a constructor
-- of this datatype, a second arm for a constructor that already has one, and an
-- arm for a case the specialiser closed — the third is
-- `vanished-case-takes-no-body`, below, because only the walk over constructors
-- knows which cases vanished.
--
-- NEITHER WALK KNOWS WHICH MISTAKE IT IS CATCHING, which is the point: each is
-- one test over the arms the user wrote, and the refusal is whichever test did
-- not hold.

-- An arm's head names a constructor of the family being split. `pattern-head`
-- answers a `Name`, `constructor-datatype` takes one, so the two compose — and a
-- head that is no constructor at all stops in `constructor-datatype`, naming the
-- word the user wrote.
arm-must-be-for-this-datatype : Name -> Name -> ()
rule arm-must-be-for-this-datatype a b :- when (same-name a b) do

arms-are-for-this-datatype : Surface -> Name -> ()
rule arms-are-for-this-datatype t d :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-no-arms t)
  do
rule arms-are-for-this-datatype t d :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-arms t)
  do p = split-arm-pattern t
     ; w = pattern-head p
     ; d2 = constructor-datatype w
     ; arm-must-be-for-this-datatype d2 d
     ; r = split-tail t
     ; arms-are-for-this-datatype r d

-- One arm per constructor. `pick` answers with the first match, so a second arm
-- for the same constructor is never reached — an unfinished edit, and silent
-- until this walk.
one-arm-per-constructor : Name -> Name -> ()
rule one-arm-per-constructor a b :- when (different-name a b) do

arm-not-repeated : Name -> Surface -> ()
rule arm-not-repeated w t :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-no-arms t)
  do
rule arm-not-repeated w t :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-arms t)
  do p = split-arm-pattern t
     ; w2 = pattern-head p
     ; one-arm-per-constructor w w2
     ; r = split-tail t
     ; arm-not-repeated w r

-- EACH HEAD AGAINST THE TAIL, not against a set: the arms are a list and
-- `split-tail` peels one, so looking forward costs nothing to carry. Two walks
-- rather than one accumulator, which is `same-length`'s shape for the same
-- reason — there is no arithmetic and no state.
arms-are-distinct : Surface -> ()
rule arms-are-distinct t :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-no-arms t)
  do
rule arms-are-distinct t :- when (surface-is-split t) (split-has-arms t)
  do p = split-arm-pattern t
     ; w = pattern-head p
     ; r = split-tail t
     ; arm-not-repeated w r
     ; arms-are-distinct r

-- The constructors and their method holes, walked in step. constructors-of and
-- prim-eliminate both answer in declaration order, so the two lists correspond
-- without either being told.
--
-- `specialise` IS TOLD WHICH HYPOTHESES THE METHOD CARRIES rather than reading
-- them off the declaration, because the declaration cannot say which scheme was
-- asked for — `caseD`'s methods carry none, and counting them from the
-- constructor made `case … of` refuse its own methods.
split-cases : Surface -> List Name -> List Core -> (List Name, Int) -> ()
rule split-cases t [] [] m :- do
rule split-cases t [c, ...cs] [mh, ...ms] m :-
  do goto mh
     ; ihs = ihs-for t c
     ; (sub, (forced, refined)) = specialise c ihs m
     ; one-case t c sub forced m refined
     ; goto mh
     ; solve
     ; split-cases t cs ms m

-- The pair prim-eliminate answers, taken apart. `m` is what the motive added to
-- every method — the ambient entries it generalised and the equation count —
-- and it is threaded whole, because only `specialise` and `intro-refined` have
-- any business in it.
split-over : Surface -> List Name -> (List Core, (List Name, Int)) -> ()
rule split-over t cs (hs, m) :- do split-cases t cs hs m

-- WHICH ELIMINATOR THE WRITTEN WORD ASKS FOR, and the only place the two
-- differ before a method is in hand. `prim-eliminate` builds an Eliminate node
-- whose methods carry their inductive hypotheses; `prim-case` applies the
-- generated caseD, whose methods are the same ones without them. Both answer
-- the same pair — the method holes, and what the motive added to each — so all
-- downstream is shared.
--
-- An arm-less split goes through here too, so `case e of { }` really applies
-- caseD and `induction e of { }` really eliminates. Nothing downstream can
-- tell, and neither can the kernel: caseD δ-unfolds to an elimination.
split-claim : Surface -> Core -> (List Core, (List Name, Int))
rule split-claim t x :- when (split-is-induction t)
  do r = prim-eliminate x
     ; return r
rule split-claim t x :- when (split-is-cases t)
  do r = prim-case x
     ; return r

-- The whole of a split, arms or none. The target's type is not known before the
-- target is elaborated, so Tsplit stands for it and unification solves it on
-- the way past — :infer's manoeuvre written as a rule. `here` is taken first
-- because `goto` is the only exact way back to a hole, and after the two claims
-- the focus has moved.
split-elaborate : Surface -> ()
rule split-elaborate t :-
  do h = here
     ; u = fresh-universe
     ; ty = claim "Tsplit" u
     ; x = claim "xsplit" ty
     ; goto x
     ; g = split-target t
     ; call elaborate g
     ; goto h
     ; r = split-claim t x
     ; tt = typeof x
     ; d = datatype-of tt
     ; cs = constructors-of d
     ; arms-are-for-this-datatype t d
     ; arms-are-distinct t
     ; split-over t cs r
     ; goto h
     ; solve

-- E⟦induction t of { p -> e ; … }⟧ and E⟦case t of { p -> e ; … }⟧ — the two
-- clauses, and they are two only because the head language has no disjunction.
-- The word is read inside, by `split-claim` and by `ihs-for`, which is where it
-- means something.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-split t) (split-has-arms t) (split-is-induction t)
  do split-elaborate t

rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-split t) (split-has-arms t) (split-is-cases t)
  do split-elaborate t

-- E⟦case t of { }⟧ and E⟦induction t of { }⟧ — A SPLIT WITH NO ARMS IS NOT A
-- SECOND MECHANISM, and it stopped being one when the specialiser arrived. It
-- is the same walk over the same methods, where every one of them is required
-- to vanish: a datatype with no constructors has no methods at all, and a
-- datatype with constructors whose every method a conflicting equation closes
-- has none left either. The two are one thing, and `split-cases` already walks
-- an empty list.
--
-- So this clause only differs in its guard, and the guard is what says the two
-- words cannot differ here: with no method surviving there is nothing for an
-- inductive hypothesis to be attached to, so `induction` and `case` agree. A
-- method that does NOT vanish finds no arm and the split is refused, which is
-- coverage failing and is the right answer.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-split t) (split-has-no-arms t)
  do split-elaborate t

-- E⟦(elim D … t) ⃗a⟧ — an elimination applied to something more, which is how a
-- proof discharges the equation an unfriendly index carries:
-- `elim Step () P (…) (true u) s (refl Term true)`. The head is the elim node
-- rather than a name, so without this the binary clause below would take it —
-- and it claims f : A -> B, an arrow, where an eliminator's type is a
-- telescope. elim-spine folds the extra arguments into the eliminator's own
-- spine, so what is elaborated is one name-headed application.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-app t) (app-head-is-elim t)
  do a = elim-spine t
     ; call elaborate a

-- E⟦x ⃗a⟧ — Brady's OTHER application rule, taken whenever the head is a name.
-- The binary rule below claims f : A -> B, an arrow, so B cannot mention the
-- argument and a dependent head does not fit it; this one walks the head's real
-- telescope, claiming each domain in the scope of the holes already claimed.
--
-- expand-implicits is Brady's EXPAND: it writes a _ in at every implicit
-- position the user left out, so from here on every argument is written and the
-- walk is the same for all of them.
--
-- **Its failure is now the answer, and before phase 170 it was thrown away.**
-- The clause used to be allowed to fall through to the binary one, on the
-- reasoning that a head whose arguments do not line up is an ordinary binary
-- application. It is not: this clause's walk takes a partial application of a
-- head with recorded plicities perfectly well, and the fall-through only ever
-- served to replace a real error with the binary clause's own. The binary
-- clause claims f : A -> B, so against a dependent head it fails on that claim
-- before it re-reaches the argument that was wrong — which is why
-- `refl String "y"` against `Eq String "x" "x"` said `A2 is not bound before A`
-- instead of naming the mismatch.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-app t) (app-head-is-name t)
  do h  = here
     ; e  = expand-implicits t
     ; hd = app-head e
     ; w  = surface-name hd
     ; f  = resolve-name w
     ; call spine-arguments h f e

-- E⟦e a⟧ — the binary rule, a spine folded right to left. CLAIM (A : Type);
-- CLAIM (B : Type); CLAIM (f : A -> B); CLAIM (s : A); FILL (f s); FOCUS f;
-- E⟦e⟧; FOCUS s; E⟦a⟧; SOLVE.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-app t) (app-head-is-term t) (app-args-are-explicit t)
  do h  = here
     ; dn = fresh-name "A"
     ; u1 = fresh-universe
     ; d  = claim dn u1
     ; cn = fresh-name "B"
     ; u2 = fresh-universe
     ; c  = claim cn u2
     ; ar = arrow d c
     ; fn = fresh-name "f"
     ; f  = claim fn ar
     ; sn = fresh-name "s"
     ; sv = claim sn d
     ; ap = apply-to f sv
     ; fill ap
     ; goto f
     ; g  = app-function t
     ; call elaborate g
     ; goto sv
     ; a  = app-last-argument t
     ; call elaborate a
     ; goto h
     ; solve
-- E⟦\ x => e⟧ = ATTACK; LAMBDA x; E⟦e⟧; SOLVE — Brady's λ case, with one
-- prim-lambda per binder of the group and one attack for the whole of it.
--
-- **The group is walked by recursion**, not by an iterating instruction: each
-- helper peels one binder off the surface term and calls itself on the tail, so
-- the term is its own counter. Attacking once per binder instead would nest the
-- λs and put an extra let round each — a different proof term, which is why the
-- run is emitted here rather than by peeling in this clause.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-lambda t)
  do h = here
     ; prim-attack
     ; into
     ; k = here
     ; back
     ; body = call intro-binders t k
     ; goto body
     ; b = lambda-body t
     ; call elaborate b
     ; goto h
     ; solve

-- E⟦(x : t) -> e⟧ = ATTACK; CLAIM (X : Type); PI (x : X); FOCUS X; E⟦t⟧; E⟦e⟧;
-- SOLVE. One binder per clause and a group nests: the domain hole is claimed
-- outside the attack, where an earlier binder of the same group is not in
-- scope. The domain is elaborated before the body — Brady's order, and not a
-- preference: the other way round the body's FILL solves the domain hole.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-forall t)
  do x  = forall-name t
     ; ty = forall-domain t
     ; tl = forall-tail t
     ; h  = here
     ; dn = fresh-name "A"
     ; u  = fresh-universe
     ; d  = claim dn u
     ; prim-attack
     ; quantify x d
     ; into
     ; along
     ; c  = here
     ; goto d
     ; call elaborate ty
     ; goto c
     ; call elaborate tl
     ; goto h
     ; solve
-- E⟦A -> B⟧ — the application case's shape with `arrow` where that has
-- `apply-to`. Not the ∀ case with an anonymous binder: an arrow's codomain
-- cannot mention the domain, so there is nothing to put in Γ and no attack.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-arrow t)
  do h  = here
     ; dn = fresh-name "A"
     ; u1 = fresh-universe
     ; d  = claim dn u1
     ; cn = fresh-name "B"
     ; u2 = fresh-universe
     ; c  = claim cn u2
     ; ar = arrow d c
     ; fill ar
     ; goto d
     ; a  = arrow-domain t
     ; call elaborate a
     ; goto c
     ; b  = arrow-codomain t
     ; call elaborate b
     ; goto h
     ; solve
-- E⟦let x = v in e⟧ — `define` is Brady's LET, and there is no attack and no
-- solve: his LET acts on the goal, ours writes a component above the focus, so
-- the body elaborates into the hole this clause was called at.
--
-- Two clauses, because the annotation is optional and the head language has no
-- negation. With one, X is left for the value's own FILL to unify against.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (let-is-annotated t)
  do h  = here
     ; xn = fresh-name "X"
     ; u  = fresh-universe
     ; x  = claim xn u
     ; vn = fresh-name "V"
     ; v  = claim vn x
     ; goto x
     ; ty = let-type t
     ; call elaborate ty
     ; goto v
     ; val = let-value t
     ; call elaborate val
     ; goto h
     ; w  = let-name t
     ; define w v
     ; b  = let-body t
     ; call elaborate b

rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (let-is-bare t)
  do h  = here
     ; xn = fresh-name "X"
     ; u  = fresh-universe
     ; x  = claim xn u
     ; vn = fresh-name "V"
     ; v  = claim vn x
     ; goto v
     ; val = let-value t
     ; call elaborate val
     ; goto h
     ; w  = let-name t
     ; define w v
     ; b  = let-body t
     ; call elaborate b
-- E⟦e : T⟧ — claim X : Type, elaborate T into it, unify X with the goal, then
-- elaborate e at the same hole. An ascription does not build a term; it narrows
-- the one the goal was already asking for, so there is no fill and no solve.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-ascription t)
  do h  = here
     ; xn = fresh-name "X"
     ; u  = fresh-universe
     ; x  = claim xn u
     ; goto x
     ; ty = ascription-type t
     ; call elaborate ty
     ; goto h
     ; g  = goal
     ; unify-into x g
     ; e  = ascription-term t
     ; call elaborate e
-- E⟦trusted T e⟧ — claim C : Type for the type claimed, elaborate T into it;
-- claim A : Type for the type the body turns out to have and B : A for the body,
-- and elaborate e into that; then build trusted C A B and fill the goal with it.
--
-- THE FILL IS LAST, AND IT IS THE ONE CLAUSE WHERE IT HAS TO BE. Everywhere else
-- the fill comes first, because filling last leaves the argument holes already
-- solved and the gotos find nothing (phases 41e and 44). Here the opposite
-- happens: a trusted node's type IS C, so `fill` unifies C with the goal and
-- SOLVES it — and `goto C` then fails with "that names no hole or guess". The
-- arrow clause can fill first because an arrow's type is a universe and not
-- either of its halves. Found by running the clause, not by reasoning about it.
--
-- `trusted _ e` needs nothing special all the same, and that is the ruling of
-- 2026-10-05 holding: the placeholder elaborates by not elaborating, so C is
-- still a hole when the fill arrives and the fill solves it from the goal,
-- exactly as it solves any other hole.
--
-- A is what the user never writes. Nothing here determines it; the body's own
-- elaboration does, by filling something whose type unification sets A to. If
-- the body leaves it open, A stays a hole, and that is the ordinary outcome of
-- elaborating something underdetermined rather than a failure of this clause.
--
-- The claim is elaborated before the body so that a claimed type mentioning
-- names the body also mentions is already in place when the body is checked.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-trusted t)
  do h  = here
     ; cn = fresh-name "C"
     ; u1 = fresh-universe
     ; c  = claim cn u1
     ; goto c
     ; ty = trusted-claim t
     ; call elaborate ty
     ; goto h
     ; an = fresh-name "A"
     ; u2 = fresh-universe
     ; a  = claim an u2
     ; bn = fresh-name "B"
     ; b  = claim bn a
     ; goto b
     ; e  = trusted-body t
     ; call elaborate e
     ; goto h
     ; tr = make-trusted c a b
     ; fill tr
     ; solve
-- E⟦elim D ⃗p P ⃗m ⃗i t⟧ — an ordinary application of the eliminator's wrapper.
--
-- §3.7 generates a global for the eliminator the way it does for every former
-- and constructor, so `elim Nat (…) P (…) (…) n` and `elimNat (…) P … n` are
-- the same term and this clause is the rewriting of one into the other. There
-- is nothing here about eliminations that is not about applications.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-elim t)
  do a = elim-spine t
     ; call elaborate a
-- E⟦do { … }⟧ = play the block. It leaves the focus wherever the block left
-- it, which is the one way it differs from every other clause: the block does
-- what the user wrote, and repairing it is theirs.
rule elaborate t :- when focus-is-hole (surface-is-do t) do play t

-- A module's own top-level do block, played where it stands among the
-- declarations (MS6 phase 104b). The driver calls this rule rather than
-- splicing the block's instructions into the module's program, so how a module
-- treats its blocks is written here, in user space, and can be replaced or
-- added to like any other rule.
--
-- It has no head test. A top-level block has no goal and nothing to take
-- apart: there is a block and it is played. A clause of your own may test
-- whatever it likes and will be offered first if it is written above this one.
run-block : Surface -> ()
rule run-block t :- do play t

-- One prim-lambda per binder, each given the SURFACE name: without it the binder
-- keeps the identifier written in the type.
--
-- **The hole for what is left of the goal is carried along.** A binder with a
-- written type has that type elaborated inside the binders already introduced —
-- the only place an earlier binder of the same group is in scope — and this hole
-- is how the clause gets there. @prim-lambda@ answers with the next one.
-- **It answers with the hole the body goes in**, which is the last binder's own
-- answer. Counting one @along@ per binder from the top of the guess was how the
-- λ case used to get there, and that is only right while the binders are the
-- only components in the chain — which stopped being true the moment a written
-- type needed a hole of its own inside them.
intro-binders : Surface -> Core -> Core
rule intro-binders t k :- when focus-is-guess (lambda-binds-more t) (lambda-is-bare t)
  do x  = lambda-name t
     ; k2 = prim-lambda x
     ; tl = lambda-tail t
     ; body = call intro-binders tl k2
     ; return body

rule intro-binders t k :- when focus-is-guess (lambda-binds-one t) (lambda-is-bare t)
  do x = lambda-name t
     ; k2 = prim-lambda x
     ; return k2

-- A written type on a binder is a CHECK, not a declaration. The binder's type is
-- still the one the goal supplies, so an annotated binder gives the same term as
-- a bare one; what was written is elaborated into a hole claimed inside the
-- binders already introduced, and then unified with the type the binder actually
-- got. So `\ A (a : A) -> a` works, a wrong annotation fails at the binder it was
-- written on, and where the goal's domain is still open the annotation solves it.
rule intro-binders t k :- when focus-is-guess (lambda-binds-more t) (lambda-is-annotated t)
  do g  = here
     ; x  = lambda-name t
     ; ty = lambda-annot-type t
     ; dn = fresh-name "A"
     ; u  = fresh-universe
     ; goto k
     ; d  = claim dn u
     ; goto d
     ; call elaborate ty
     ; goto g
     ; k2 = prim-lambda x
     ; goto k2
     ; v  = resolve-name x
     ; s  = typeof v
     ; unify d s
     ; goto g
     ; tl = lambda-tail t
     ; body = call intro-binders tl k2
     ; return body

rule intro-binders t k :- when focus-is-guess (lambda-binds-one t) (lambda-is-annotated t)
  do g  = here
     ; x  = lambda-name t
     ; ty = lambda-annot-type t
     ; dn = fresh-name "A"
     ; u  = fresh-universe
     ; goto k
     ; d  = claim dn u
     ; goto d
     ; call elaborate ty
     ; goto g
     ; k2 = prim-lambda x
     ; goto k2
     ; v  = resolve-name x
     ; s  = typeof v
     ; unify d s
     ; goto g
     ; return k2

-- The walk itself, and it is a recursion over the surface spine: take the first
-- argument, claim a hole for it, elaborate into that hole, and call on what is
-- left. The term is its own counter, exactly as the λ group is at
-- intro-binders — which is what lets a rule apply a head to any number of
-- arguments without ever holding a list (ms4/CLOSEOUT.md 28).
--
-- **goto h before each claim.** Every claim goes directly above the focus, so
-- claiming from the one fixed place is what puts the domains in telescope order
-- and lets a later domain mention an earlier hole.
--
-- focus-is-component is there for enter-binders' reason (phase 49c): a head of
-- argument tests alone is true of every state, so the clause would be offered
-- everywhere. It is a test that is TRUE here, not one chosen to tidy the match
-- list — the focus is the hole on the way in and the argument just elaborated
-- on the way round.
spine-arguments : Core -> Core -> Surface -> ()
rule spine-arguments h f t :- when focus-is-component (surface-is-app t)
  do goto h
     ; n  = fresh-name "a"
     ; f2 = apply-next f n
     ; goto-named n
     ; a  = app-first-argument t
     ; call elaborate a
     ; tl = app-tail t
     ; call spine-arguments h f2 tl

-- The base case: the arguments are gone and what is left is the head. Only now
-- is the spine filled in — the arguments are elaborated BEFORE the FILL,
-- against Brady's printed order, because unifying the spine's type with the
-- goal solves the argument holes and a solved hole is a definition, so a goto
-- afterwards would find nothing.
rule spine-arguments h f t :- when focus-is-component (surface-is-name t)
  do goto h
     ; fill f
     ; solve
